Cloud gaming platforms frequently suffer from latency spikes, jitter, and visual stutter caused by distant data centers, standard operating system networking delays, and wireless packet loss. 
This project presents an edge-native architecture designed to guarantee sub-millisecond state recovery and ultra-low latency for real-time multiplayer games.
By deploying authoritative game servers on AWS Wavelength (directly inside 5G telecom hubs), implementing eBPF/XDP for driver-level kernel bypass routing, and integrating a linear-time Mamba Selective State Space Model (SSM), the system detects dropped UDP packets at the network card level and instantly predicts missing 3D player trajectories. 
This approach maintains a synchronized, authoritative game state at the edge without sacrificing video quality or overloading network bandwidth.
